// Parallel loops: a range of work spread over the actors' workers (the
// package's header, PARALLEL LOOPS). This is the LOW-LEVEL form: a function
// reference, a pointer to shared data and an index range, called as a
// library. The language's 'parallel each' will be written over it, and the
// rules it will check at compile time (below) are for now the caller's.
//
//   import actors;
//
//   struct Sums {
//     in *u64;                  // read by every piece
//     out *u64;                 // piece i writes only out[i]
//   }
//
//   fn square(data *u8, lo usize, hi usize) {
//     imm d = data as *Sums;
//     mut i = lo;
//     while i < hi {
//       *((*d).out + i) = *((*d).in + i) * *((*d).in + i);
//       i = i + 1usize;
//     }
//   }
//
//   fn main() {
//     initAll();
//     mut s = new Sums(inp, outp);
//     actors.parallelRange(0usize, 1000000usize, 1024usize, &square, &mut s as *u8);
//     // every out[i] is written; the loop has finished
//     finalAll();
//   }
//
//   actors.parallelRange(lo, hi, grain, body, data)
//                          run body over [lo, hi), in pieces no smaller than
//                          grain (except the last of a range that does not
//                          divide), and return when all have finished
//   actors.parallelRangeThen(lo, hi, grain, body, data, onDone, doneArg)
//                          the same, but return at once: whichever piece
//                          finishes last calls onDone(doneArg), on whichever
//                          thread that is. This is the form a loop inside an
//                          actor's behaviour will end at.
//   actors.RangeBody       &fn(data *u8, lo usize, hi usize)
//
// WHAT THE BODY MAY DO. This form TRUSTS ITS CALLER. The body is called with
// the caller's 'data' pointer and a sub-range [lo, hi) of the loop's range,
// any number of times, on any worker, in any order, and at the same time as
// itself on other pieces. So it must write only the items of its own
// sub-range (or its own locals); it must read what it shares only if no piece
// writes it; and it must not 'await', or block on anything another piece or
// the loop's caller must do (a piece is run to completion on the worker's
// stack, and a loop that waited could not finish). It may call
// parallelRange itself: the inner loop finishes inside the piece that began
// it. It may send messages (their order across pieces is not defined). A
// body that breaks these rules races: nothing here checks it, and 'parallel
// each' will refuse such a body where it is written.
//
// SPLIT ON DEMAND (Cilk, TBB, Rayon). The loop starts as ONE task, the whole
// range [lo, hi), which the caller runs itself. Whoever runs a task splits it
// in half while it is longer than grain: one half is put on top of the queue
// of tasks of the worker running it, and the other is split further the same
// way, until a piece no longer than grain is run. An idle worker steals the
// OLDEST task in someone's queue, which is the largest, and splits it in
// turn. Nothing is divided by the number of CPUs ahead of time: uneven work
// spreads itself, and with no thief the loop costs about a plain loop and a
// few pushes. Tasks are values in a queue (a range and the loop's record),
// not allocations.
//
// WHERE TASKS WAIT. A worker has, besides the ring of actors that
// messages made runnable, a deque of tasks (TaskDeque), and takes in this
// order: its own tasks, newest first (the small piece next to the one it just
// ran, so that its cache is warm); the tasks of a loop begun off the workers;
// its own actors, then the inject queue's; and only when its own queue is
// empty it steals: another worker's OLDEST task, and only if there is none,
// another's actors. So a task sits on top of the queue, above the actors'
// waiting messages, and stays on top until it is done; and a thief prefers
// tasks to actors. A deque holds 128 tasks; one that is full is not split any
// further (the piece in hand is run whole), which a loop's splitting depth
// reaches only in nested loops many levels deep.
//
// COMPLETION. The loop's record counts the items not yet run, atomically. A
// task that has run its piece subtracts the piece's length; the task whose
// subtraction brings the count to zero is the last. In the scheduled form
// (parallelRangeThen) the last task calls onDone: nothing polls, and nobody
// is woken. The blocking form (parallelRange) has the caller WORK while it
// waits: it runs tasks (its own newest first, then ones stolen from anywhere)
// until the count is zero, and yields its processor when there is none to
// run, since the pieces still running are on other workers. So a loop called
// from main, from a thread of its own, from a worker's own code or from inside
// an actor's behaviour all end the same way. It is not a sleeping wait: a
// caller waiting on a long piece is a busy yield-loop for as long as that
// piece runs.
//
// OUTSIDE AND INSIDE AN ACTOR. Called from a thread that is not a worker (main
// is one), the loop starts the workers if no actor has (the same lazy start;
// finalAll() stops them, as it does for actors), and its tasks go on a deque
// shared by all such callers (the shared deque of Sched, extTasks), which the
// workers look at before their actors. Called from a worker -- from inside an
// actor's behaviour, or from the body of a loop -- its tasks go on that
// worker's own deque, and the worker, which is running the behaviour, runs
// tasks meanwhile instead of actors: the actor whose behaviour called it is
// not run again until the loop has finished, because its behaviour has not
// returned (its mailbox goes on accepting messages meanwhile, and they are
// handled afterwards, in order). A worker waiting in a loop runs only tasks,
// never another actor, so waiting does not nest actors on one stack.
//
// A loop begun with parallelRangeThen from a worker puts its first task on
// that worker's deque, and from any other thread on the shared one. It is
// counted (liveScheduled) so that finalAll() does not stop the workers while
// it is unfinished.

// The body of a loop: the caller's data pointer and a sub-range [lo, hi) of
// the range, called possibly many times and at once on different pieces
pub alias RangeBody = &fn(data *u8, lo usize, hi usize);

// How many tasks a deque holds
const taskCap = 128u64;
const taskMask = 127u64;

// One loop, shared by its tasks: the caller's stack for the blocking form, a
// heap block for the scheduled form, freed by the last task. Every field but
// 'remaining' is written before the first task runs and only read afterwards;
// a task reads what it needs from it before its own subtraction, since the
// caller of a blocking loop may return the instant the count reaches zero.
struct LoopRec {
  pub remaining usize;         // items not yet run (atomic)
  pub grain usize;
  pub body RangeBody;
  pub data *u8;
  pub onDone &fn(arg *u8);     // called by the last task, scheduled form only
  pub doneArg *u8;
  pub scheduled bool;          // a heap block, with an onDone to call
}

// A task: a range of a loop. It is a plain value; a deque holds copies.
struct Task {
  pub rec *LoopRec;
  pub lo usize;
  pub hi usize;
}

// A queue of tasks under a lock (a Mutex per worker, held for a few
// instructions at a time): the owner pushes and takes at the newest end,
// thieves take at the oldest. 'n' is how many it holds, written under the
// lock, and read without it where a look is enough.
struct TaskDeque {
  pub lock sync.Mutex;
  pub n u64;
  pub head u64;                // the position of the oldest, counting up for ever
  pub items Array[Task, 128];
}

fn nullRec() *LoopRec {null;}

fn emptyTask() Task {new Task(nullRec(), 0usize, 0usize);}

fn noDone(arg *u8) {}

fn tasksOf(w *Worker) *TaskDeque {&(*w).tasks as *TaskDeque;}

fn extDeque(s *Sched) *TaskDeque {&(*s).extTasks as *TaskDeque;}

fn taskAt(d *TaskDeque, pos u64) *Task {
  (&(*d).items as *Task) + usize.from(pos & taskMask);
}

// Put t at the newest end of d: false, with nothing put, if d is full
fn dequePush(d *TaskDeque, t Task) bool {
  (*d).lock.lock();
  imm n = (*d).n;
  if n >= taskCap {
    (*d).lock.unlock();
    return false;
  }
  imm slot = taskAt(d, (*d).head + n);
  *slot = t;
  mem.atomicStore[u64](&(*d).n as *u64, n + 1u64, MemOrder.SeqCst[]);
  (*d).lock.unlock();
  true;
}

// Take the newest task of d into *out: false if there is none
fn dequePopNewest(d *TaskDeque, out *Task) bool {
  if ld64(&(*d).n as *u64) == 0u64 {
    return false;
  }
  (*d).lock.lock();
  imm n = (*d).n;
  if n == 0u64 {
    (*d).lock.unlock();
    return false;
  }
  *out = *taskAt(d, (*d).head + n - 1u64);
  mem.atomicStore[u64](&(*d).n as *u64, n - 1u64, MemOrder.SeqCst[]);
  (*d).lock.unlock();
  true;
}

// Take the oldest task of d, the largest, into *out: false if there is none
fn dequeTakeOldest(d *TaskDeque, out *Task) bool {
  if ld64(&(*d).n as *u64) == 0u64 {
    return false;
  }
  (*d).lock.lock();
  imm n = (*d).n;
  if n == 0u64 {
    (*d).lock.unlock();
    return false;
  }
  *out = *taskAt(d, (*d).head);
  (*d).head = (*d).head + 1u64;
  mem.atomicStore[u64](&(*d).n as *u64, n - 1u64, MemOrder.SeqCst[]);
  (*d).lock.unlock();
  true;
}

// Whether any deque holds a task, looked at without taking one
fn taskWork(s *Sched) bool {
  if ld64(&(*s).extTasks.n as *u64) != 0u64 {
    return true;
  }
  mut i = 0u32;
  while i < (*s).nworkers {
    if ld64(&(*workerAt(s, i)).tasks.n as *u64) != 0u64 {
      return true;
    }
    i = i + 1u32;
  }
  false;
}

// The deque of the caller's own tasks: its worker's, or the shared one of a
// thread that is not a worker of this runtime
fn curDeque(s *Sched) *TaskDeque {
  imm w = me as *Worker;
  if w as usize != 0usize and (*w).sched as usize == s as usize {
    return tasksOf(w);
  }
  extDeque(s);
}

// Take the oldest task of another worker's deque, or of the shared one: into
// *out, false if there is none. w is the thief if it is a worker, so that it
// does not look in its own deque; its look begins at a worker that moves on
// at every steal.
fn stealTask(s *Sched, w *Worker, out *Task) bool {
  imm n = (*s).nworkers;
  imm r = mem.atomicAdd[u32](&(*s).stealRot as *u32, 1u32, MemOrder.Relaxed[]);
  mut i = 0u32;
  while i < n {
    imm v = workerAt(s, (r + i) % n);
    if v as usize != w as usize {
      if dequeTakeOldest(tasksOf(v), out) {
        mem.atomicAdd[u64](&(*s).taskSteals as *u64, 1u64, MemOrder.Relaxed[]);
        return true;
      }
    }
    i = i + 1u32;
  }
  if dequeTakeOldest(extDeque(s), out) {
    mem.atomicAdd[u64](&(*s).taskSteals as *u64, 1u64, MemOrder.Relaxed[]);
    return true;
  }
  false;
}

// Run task t: split it while it is longer than the loop's grain, putting the
// upper half of each split on top of this thread's deque; run the piece left;
// count it done
fn runTask(s *Sched, t Task) {
  imm rec = t.rec;
  imm body = (*rec).body;
  imm data = (*rec).data;
  imm grain = (*rec).grain;
  imm scheduled = (*rec).scheduled;
  imm onDone = (*rec).onDone;
  imm doneArg = (*rec).doneArg;
  imm d = curDeque(s);
  mut hi = t.hi;
  while hi - t.lo > grain {
    imm mid = t.lo + (hi - t.lo) / 2usize;
    if !dequePush(d, new Task(rec, mid, hi)) {
      break;
    }
    notify(s);
    hi = mid;
  }
  body(data, t.lo, hi);
  finishItems(s, rec, hi - t.lo, scheduled, onDone, doneArg);
}

// The items of one piece are done. The last piece's subtraction ends the
// loop: a scheduled loop's block is freed and its onDone called. A blocking
// loop's record may be gone as soon as the count is zero, so nothing here
// touches it after the subtraction.
fn finishItems(s *Sched, rec *LoopRec, n usize, scheduled bool, onDone &fn(arg *u8), doneArg *u8) {
  imm prev = mem.atomicSub[usize](&(*rec).remaining as *usize, n, MemOrder.AcqRel[]);
  if prev == n and scheduled {
    libc.free(rec as *u8);
    onDone(doneArg);
    mem.atomicSub[u64](&(*s).liveScheduled as *u64, 1u64, MemOrder.SeqCst[]);
    mem.atomicAdd[u32](&(*s).quiet as *u32, 1u32, MemOrder.SeqCst[]);
    sync.futexWake(&(*s).quiet);
  }
}

// The caller of a blocking loop works until its count is zero: its own newest
// task, else the oldest of anyone's; with none to take, the pieces left are
// running on other workers, so it yields its processor and looks again
fn helpUntilDone(s *Sched, rec *LoopRec) {
  imm remp = &(*rec).remaining as *usize;
  imm w = me as *Worker;
  imm mine = curDeque(s);
  while mem.atomicLoad[usize](remp, MemOrder.Acquire[]) != 0usize {
    mut t = emptyTask();
    if dequePopNewest(mine, &mut t as *Task) or stealTask(s, w, &mut t as *Task) {
      runTask(s, t);
    } else {
      yieldProcessor();
    }
  }
}

// A grain of 0 is taken as 1
fn grainOf(g usize) usize {
  if g == 0usize {1usize;} else {g;}
}

// Run body over the range [lo, hi) of indexes, in pieces of at least grain
// items (a range no longer than grain is one call, made at once on the
// caller's thread, with nothing else started) on the workers of the actors'
// scheduler, and return when every piece has finished. An empty range
// (hi <= lo) calls nothing. The caller works on its own loop while it waits.
//
// This is the low-level form: 'parallel each' will sit on it. It TRUSTS ITS
// CALLER on the body's rules: the body writes only the items of the sub-range
// it is handed, reads shared data that no piece writes, and does not 'await'
// or wait on another piece (this file's header, WHAT THE BODY MAY DO). It may
// be called from main, from any thread, or from inside an actor's behaviour,
// where the actor is not run again until it returns; the workers are started
// if no actor has started them.
pub fn parallelRange(lo usize, hi usize, grain usize, body RangeBody, data *u8) {
  if hi <= lo {
    return;
  }
  imm g = grainOf(grain);
  if hi - lo <= g {
    body(data, lo, hi);
    return;
  }
  ensureRunning();
  imm s = running as *Sched;
  mut rec = new LoopRec(hi - lo, g, body, data, &noDone, null, false);
  imm p = &mut rec as *LoopRec;
  runTask(s, new Task(p, lo, hi));
  helpUntilDone(s, p);
}

// The same loop, started and left: it returns at once, and the piece that
// finishes last calls onDone(doneArg), on whichever thread runs it, exactly
// once; for an empty range, or one no longer than grain, onDone is called
// before this returns, after the single call of body for the latter. The
// first task goes on top of the caller's own queue if it is a worker, else on
// the shared deque, and workers take it from there. data must live until
// onDone is called. The same trust in the body's rules as parallelRange.
pub fn parallelRangeThen(lo usize, hi usize, grain usize, body RangeBody, data *u8,
    onDone &fn(arg *u8), doneArg *u8) {
  imm g = grainOf(grain);
  if hi <= lo or hi - lo <= g {
    if hi > lo {
      body(data, lo, hi);
    }
    onDone(doneArg);
    return;
  }
  ensureRunning();
  imm s = running as *Sched;
  imm rec = libc.malloc(mem.sizeof[LoopRec]()) as *LoopRec;
  if rec as usize == 0usize {
    die("actors: out of memory for a parallel loop");
  }
  mem.writeRaw[LoopRec](rec, new LoopRec(hi - lo, g, body, data, onDone, doneArg, true));
  mem.atomicAdd[u64](&(*s).liveScheduled as *u64, 1u64, MemOrder.SeqCst[]);
  if !dequePush(curDeque(s), new Task(rec, lo, hi)) {
    // The deque is full: run the loop's first task here, which splits it
    runTask(s, new Task(rec, lo, hi));
    return;
  }
  notify(s);
}
